\section*{Single-lattice covering density of order n log n}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that every convex body in dimension at least two admits a single-lattice covering of density at most an absolute multiple of $n\log n$.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
An absolute constant $C>0$ is claimed such that for every dimension $n\ge2$ and every convex body $K\subset\mathbb R^n$ there is one full-rank lattice $\Lambda$ with
\[
 K+\Lambda=\mathbb R^n,\qquad |K|/\det\Lambda\le Cn\log n.
\]
The ratio is lattice covering density. Neither central symmetry nor smooth boundary is needed, and the covering uses one lattice rather than a union of independently translated lattices.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23), a hierarchy of branching lattice blocks supplies usable anchors. Folded Gaussian sampling and simultaneous shears cover a finite collection of horizontal patterns, after which a final small-hole removal produces an exact covering by one lattice.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: every convex body in dimension at least two admits a single-lattice covering of density at most an absolute multiple of $n\log n$.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{CoveringDensity.lean}, \texttt{SingleLatticeCovering.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CoveringOrder.optimal\_order}, \texttt{OAI.SingleLatticeCovering.single\_lattice\_covering}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

